1) Encapsulation — hide internal data, expose safe operations
----------------Encapsulation----------------
Encapsulation in Javascript describes the concept of bundling data and methods within a single unit.
●we can achieve encapsulation in Javascript by using classes and packages
advantages
1. it provides security
2. it provides modularity in our programs
disadvantages
1. It increases the length of the program.

What it is (one line): Keep data and the methods that change it together; prevent outsiders from directly touching internal details.


In code: Order uses private fields #items and #discount. Callers use applyDiscount(), addItem(), calculateTotal() — they cannot modify #items directly.


Why useful: Prevents other parts of the program from accidentally corrupting order data (e.g., changing price directly).


Analogy: A bank account — you deposit/withdraw using methods; you can’t directly change the balance.


2) Abstraction — provide a simple interface, hide complexity
---------------Abstraction -------------
●Abstraction is used to hide internal details and show only functionalities.
●showing necessary services or features to the end user by hiding their implementation details is called an abstraction.
-> we can achieve abstraction in Javascript by using abstract classes and interfaces.
Advantage:
1. it provides security.

What it is: Expose simple methods so callers don’t need to know internal details.


In code:


order.calculateTotal() hides how subtotal, taxes, discounts work.


PaymentGateway.charge() hides the complexity of talking to Stripe/PayPal.


Why useful: If internals change (new discount rules), only the Order class changes; other code keeps calling the same method.


Analogy: Using a TV remote — you press “volume up” without needing to know how the circuit works.


