Practical content for each pillar (short checklist & tips)
Encapsulation — checklist
Use private fields (e.g., #items) or clearly documented private properties.


Provide methods for allowed actions (applyDiscount, addItem).


Don’t expose direct setters for sensitive fields like price/payment status.


Common mistake: Using global variables or exporting internal arrays directly.


Abstraction — checklist
Keep complex logic inside methods (calculateTotal()).


Controllers/services should call high-level methods only.


Common mistake: Repeating low-level logic across controllers.


Inheritance — checklist
Use base classes for shared contract/behavior.


Keep subclasses small: only provider-specific logic.


Common mistake: Deep inheritance chains — prefer simple base + composition.


Polymorphism — checklist
Depend on interfaces/contracts, not concrete classes (OrderService uses gateway contract).


Use dependency injection (pass gateway into constructor).


Common mistake: Using if (provider === 'stripe') ... else ... everywhere.


 Does JavaScript have real classes? How Node.js uses OOP? Why OOP?
**“JavaScript technically doesn’t have real classes like Java or C#.
**JavaScript classes are mainly for cleaner code.
 Internally, JavaScript behind the scenes still uses prototypes to reuse data and functions.
** The ‘class’ keyword in JavaScript is just syntactic sugar — behind the scenes, it still uses prototype-based inheritance
 But from a developer point of view, JavaScript classes behave just like real classes:
 we can define properties, methods, do inheritance, and create multiple objects.
Even though JavaScript is prototype-based, Node.js uses OOP everywhere internally.
 For example, Express creates ‘Request’, ‘Response’, and ‘Router’ objects for every request.
 Mongoose and Sequelize models are built using classes.
 The EventEmitter, Streams, Buffers, and File System modules all use classes.
 So OOP is actually very important in Node.js.”


What is a prototype-based language?
“A language where objects inherit from other objects, not from classes.”
 Why is JavaScript prototype-based?
Because:
In JavaScript, every object has a hidden parent object, called prototype.


When you call a method, JavaScript searches in:


That object


Its prototype


Then prototype’s prototype
 (This chain is called prototype chain)


Even when you write:
class User {}
JavaScript secretly converts it to:
function User() {}
User.prototype = { ... }

So class keyword is fake → it's a clean syntax, but internally JavaScript is still prototype based.

 Interview answer (easy English)
Here is the best answer you can tell in interviews:
“JavaScript is not class-based like Java.
 JavaScript uses prototypes — objects inherit from other objects.
 The class keyword in JavaScript is only a cleaner syntax, but behind the scenes JavaScript still uses prototypes.
 So we say JavaScript is a prototype-based language.”
class, prototype, and object creation in JavaScript step by step
=====================================================
Step 1: 
You might write:
class User {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }
  sayHi() {
    console.log(`Hi, I am ${this.name}`);
  }
}

You see class → you think it’s like Java class.


Reality: JS does not create a real class.


It only creates a function + a prototype object behind the scenes.



⭐ Step 2 — Internally, JS converts class to function + prototype
Your class:
class User {
  constructor(name) { this.name = name; }
  sayHi() { console.log(`Hi, I am ${this.name}`); }
}

Becomes internally:
function User(name) {
  this.name = name;  // this will be the object properties
}

User.prototype.sayHi = function() {
  console.log(`Hi, I am ${this.name}`);
}

Explanation in simple terms:
The constructor becomes a normal function User().


The methods inside the class (sayHi) go to User.prototype.


Every object created from User links to this prototype.



⭐ Step 3 — What happens when we use new
const user1 = new User("Ajmal");

When JS sees new User("Ajmal"), it does 4 things:
Create a new empty object

 user1 = {}


Set its prototype to User.prototype

 user1.__proto__ = User.prototype
 → Now user1 can use all methods in User.prototype like sayHi()


Run the constructor function with this pointing to user1

 this.name = "Ajmal"
 → Now user1 has property name = "Ajmal"


Return the object automatically

 return user1



⭐ Step 4 — Where the method is stored
user1.sayHi();  // Hi, I am Ajmal
sayHi() is not inside user1 itself.


JavaScript looks at user1.__proto__ (User.prototype) → finds sayHi() → executes it.


✅ This is prototype-based inheritance.
